# Chapter 13 — *Controlled Conditions*

**Bunkyō. 11:52 p.m.**

The air came out of the vent at seventeen degrees and made no sound at all.

Ami Mizuno lay on her back on the floor of her apartment with her calves hooked over the seat of her one chair and her hair fanned out on the tatami, and listened to the nothing where the tick used to be.

Six weeks ago there had been a tick. Aperiodic, maddening, roughly every nine to thirteen seconds and never twice the same interval — a beat frequency thrown off a fragment of blower guard the size of a thumbnail, riding the difference between fan speed and line frequency, and she had lost eleven nights of sleep to it before she went up on the roof with a driver set and a laptop and took the whole twenty-two-year-old unit apart to find it.

Now the air moved through her rooms in total silence, and she could not stop hearing that.

Her apartment was six mats and a kitchen that was really a hotplate with ambitions. She had chosen it on purpose. Her mother had offered the guest room with its own bath and a window that faced the garden, and Ami had signed a lease in Bunkyō instead for forty-one thousand a month, because a person who intended to spend her life telling strangers to do hard things ought to know what the hard things cost. Rent, then utilities, then transport, then food, in that order, and food was the one with the give in it. She had eaten rice and the cheap bagged sprouts for nine days running and had gotten quite good at it.

On the desk, the semester lay finished.

That was the trouble. Twenty courses across three delivery formats, four of them tested out of entirely, and this morning the last of the biochem modules had closed with a score she had not bothered to look at past the first digit. Med school entrance in the spring. Two recommendation letters already in hand, the third promised. She had built the schedule with slack in it the way you build a bridge with slack in it, for the load you cannot predict — and the load had not come, and the slack had arrived all at once, tonight, an unspent hour sitting in her chest like a held breath.

She had no idea what to do with it. Her hands wanted a task.

Which was how she came to be thinking about the unit.

It began technically, the way everything with her began technically. She thought about the control board she had reflashed, squatting on hot tarpaper with the laptop balanced on her thighs, killing the phantom defrost routine some technician in 2003 had written to paper over a sensor fault nobody had ever gone back to fix. She thought about the third-hand ingenuity of it — the hand-rewound stator, the shim cut from a license plate, whoever that maintainer had been, a person doing Fourier analysis with their hands and no instruments.

And then, without any transition she could later identify, she was thinking about his forearm.

It had been braced across the top of the housing to hold the fan cage steady while she got the driver in, and there had been a seam of scar running from wrist to elbow, old and pale and badly closed, and his whole arm had gone corded under the load and had not shaken. He had said *what are you* in a voice that had lost its footing. He had held her eyes when he thanked her, and only her eyes, for two hours, while she stood next to him in a soaked white shirt with no bra and her nipples plainly visible through it and her shorts cut off at the top of her thighs, and he had not once looked down. She had checked. She had checked four times, which was itself a finding she had declined to write up.

Ami sat up.

Her body had done something while she was not supervising it. A slow heat had come up through her, low, under the navel, and settled where it always settled, and her thighs had pressed together of their own accord, and her nipples had gone tight and hard under the oversized university sweatshirt she slept in, which had nothing to do with seventeen degrees.

*Dutton and Aron,* she thought, reflexively, the way you reach for a rail.

She had used it on herself on the roof that day and it had worked. Misattribution of arousal: subjects crossing a high suspension bridge rate the woman at the far end as more attractive than subjects crossing a low stable one, because the body arrives at the physiological state first and the mind writes the label afterward, and the label is frequently wrong. Elevated heart rate, adrenaline, thirty-four degrees, a structural failure resolved in company, two hours of shared problem-solving under time pressure. Textbook confound. She had labeled it and shelved it and it had stayed shelved.

She looked around her apartment.

Twenty-three degrees at the floor. Her pulse at rest, sixty-one, she'd checked it at eleven out of habit. No height, no danger, no deadline, no man in the room. Every variable Dutton and Aron had ever manipulated was sitting at baseline, and the effect was reproducing anyway, right now, in a still room, six weeks out, off nothing but the memory of a forearm.

"Ah," said Ami, out loud, to no one.

She had spent her life being told she was brilliant and had learned early that the compliment was mostly about speed. The thing nobody praised, because nobody saw it, was that she did not flinch from results. A result that contradicted the model was the most valuable thing in the world and you did not get to negotiate with it.

The result was in.

She stood up too fast and her vision went gray at the edges and she put a hand on the wall until it cleared, and then she got her wallet.

