**ENEOS, Hakusan-dōri. 12:19 a.m.**

The station sat on a corner where two roads met at an angle nobody had planned, four pumps under a white canopy, the light off it so bright and so cold that it made a little daytime out of thirty meters of wet asphalt and then stopped dead at the edge. Moths worked the canopy fixtures in slow spirals. The ice cabinet outside the shop door ran a compressor that cycled every four minutes and shuddered the whole housing when it kicked.

Inside smelled like three things fighting: the fryer oil from the hot case, the ammonia the night man had used on the floor at eleven, and under both of them the sweet chemical ghost of gasoline that lives in the walls of every station on earth.

Ami came in under the door chime in a gray university sweatshirt three sizes too big, sleep shorts, and plastic sandals, with her midnight-blue bob flattened on one side from the tatami and no bra and no makeup and eleven hundred yen she had decided she was allowed to spend. The sweatshirt hung to the tops of her thighs and left the rest of her legs bare and pale under the fluorescents, the calves neat and the thighs smooth and close together, swimmer's legs on a girl five foot one. The cold of the shop found her through the fleece in about four seconds and set her nipples up hard again, two small definite points on the modest swell of her tits, and she folded her arms over them without breaking stride, the way she had been folding her arms over them since she was fifteen.

She went to the drinks case for the cheap barley tea.

He was at the hot case.

She knew him from behind at nine meters, which was a datum she would examine later at length: the width of the shoulders, the height that made the shelving look like a scale model, the orange vest slung over one arm with the burn scar across the left ribs of it. Black hair, unkempt, wet at the back of the neck like he'd put his head under a tap. Boots that had left two prints on the night man's clean floor and a small apologetic trail of grit behind them.

Ami stopped with her hand on the cooler door.

Every efficient thing she had ever done in her life fell out of her at once. She stood there with the cold pouring out of the open case over her bare legs and could not construct a sentence.

He turned around holding a paper tray with two pieces of fried chicken on it and saw her, and did the same thing. Stopped flat. The tray tilted a little.

"Oh," he said.

"Ah," said Ami.

"Hi."

"Hello."

Which exhausted it. The compressor on the ice cabinet kicked outside and made them both twitch.

Byl looked at the chicken. He set the tray down on the rack, picked it back up, cleared his throat with real violence, and said, "Byl."

Ami blinked.

"That's — sorry." He gestured vaguely, a large hand indicating the general concept of himself. "Name. Byl. We spent two hours inside a rooftop unit and I never gave you it."

"Ami Mizuno," she said. And then, before she had cleared it with anyone: "Thank you for going first. I've felt badly about that for six weeks."

"About what?"

"I never asked your name." Her chin came up a fraction. "I took your unit apart in front of you and I let you buy me a can of coffee afterward and I did not ask you your name. I've thought about it more than once."

Byl started laughing.

It was not a polite laugh. It came up out of his chest and folded him over the hot case for a second, and the night man looked up from his phone, and Ami stood there with cold air pouring over her legs, entirely lost.

"No — sorry." He wiped his eye with the back of his wrist. "I never got yours either."

She opened her mouth.

"Two hours," he said. "You reflashed a control board off a laptop on a roof in thirty-four degrees and killed a defrost routine I'd been fighting for two summers, and I stood there and watched you do it, and I walked down nine flights and got all the way to the street before I worked out I didn't know what to call you. And I've been carrying that around exactly as long as you have."

"Oh," said Ami.

And something in her chest simply let go — the whole six weeks of it, the small ugly private shame of having been rude to a person who had been kind, dissolving in a gas station at twenty past midnight — and she laughed too, one clear startled syllable, with her hand up over her mouth and her seawater eyes gone wide above it.

The cooler alarm started beeping about the open door. She shut it. She had forgotten entirely to take anything out.

---

They moved along the shelves together without either of them proposing it.

He asked what she did and she told him, and watched him take it seriously, which most people did not — most people heard *nineteen* and *medical school* and produced a noise. Byl put the tray down on the shelf edge and turned around fully to face her.

"Spring entrance?"

"April. If the third letter comes."

"Off twenty courses in a year."

"Twenty-two, counting the two I tested out of in August."

He whistled low and looked genuinely, uncomplicatedly impressed, and the fluorescents were not kind to anyone but they were doing something to his face anyway, the deep blue eyes and the two days of black stubble and a fresh line of something pale and chalky still in the hair at his temple. "That's not studying. That's a campaign."

"It's scheduling," said Ami. "Most people could do it. They've simply never been shown how to lay it out."

"Most people can't do it and you know it."

"That's ungenerous to most people."

"It's generous to you. You can take it or leave it." He picked the tray back up. "I get it wrong constantly, and I've got two classes."

"What classes?"

"Metallurgy, because it pays. And a linguistics thing I took because the room had air conditioning." He shrugged with one shoulder. "Turned out to be the best thing in my week. Forty credits toward absolutely nothing."

"That's not nothing."

"It's forty credits and a lot of debt toward nothing."

"How's the work?" she asked.

"Good, actually." He said it like the word surprised him coming out. "That's new. Got a run at a shrine up in Azabu, the whole property's coming apart at once — roof, gutters, wiring, a stair tread with termites in the stringer. Eighteen items on the list and the list keeps growing. Six weeks if nothing underneath is rotten, and something underneath is always rotten." He hefted the tray. "First time in three years I've known where I'm working on a Thursday."

Ami filed that. Six weeks of steady work, in a voice that had gone careful around the good news the way people do when they have learned not to hold things loosely.

She wanted the conversation to continue. She was aware of wanting it with unusual clarity, the way she was aware of most things, and aware also that he had already picked up his chicken and half-turned toward the register, and that a man who wanted a conversation to continue does not do that.

Her heart rate was up. She noted it. Sixty-one at rest at eleven o'clock; considerably north of that now, under fluorescent light, in a shop that smelled of fryer oil, with no bridge, no height, no adrenaline, and no confound left standing anywhere in the room.

"How has your night been?" said Ami.

He stopped.

It was a small question and it landed somewhere it had no business landing. She watched him take a breath to answer it, and then not answer it, and look at the shelf of instant noodles for a beat and a half too long.

"Honestly?"

"That is the format I prefer."

"I don't understand girls at all," Byl said.

Ami filed that one somewhere deeper and considerably more permanent, with the corner turned down.

"In what sense?"

"In the sense that I don't." He rubbed the back of his neck. "Any of it. Never have."

"Do you have a girlfriend?"

He laughed.

That was the part she did not like, and she disliked it immediately and completely. It was not a deflection and it was not modesty and there was no performance in it anywhere — he laughed the short unbothered laugh of a man to whom a friend has told a small good joke, and then he glanced at her to share it, expecting to find her laughing too.

Ami did not laugh. She stood very still in her sandals with her arms folded over her tits and looked up at him and let her face do nothing at all, and after a moment his own smile came apart at the edges, the way a man's does when he discovers he has been the only one in on it.

"No," he said. "Sorry. No, I don't."

"You found that funny."

"It's — " He made a gesture that gave up halfway. "It's been over five years. Five and a half. Whatever I've got, none of them stayed in it long. Three months was the record and she spent most of the third month deciding." He shrugged again, both shoulders this time, and the shrug was practiced and old and it made something in Ami's chest hurt in a way she had not consented to. "So it's not really a live question. That's all."

*Five and a half years,* Ami filed. *None of them lasted. He believes the shortness was about him and has never once audited that belief.*

"You said 'girls,'" she said. "Plural. Just now. But you meant one."

Byl looked at her.

"That's — " He exhaled through his nose. "Yeah. All right. Yeah."

---

They reached the counter.

He put down the chicken and a small can of black coffee. Ami put down her barley tea, an egg sandwich, a rice ball, and then — after a fractional hesitation that cost her more than the money did — reached past his forearm and pushed his chicken and his coffee into the same pile, and had her card on the reader before his hand got there.

"Hey. No."

"It's done."

"Mizuno—"

"It's four hundred and ten yen." The reader chirped. The night man, who had been watching this entire transaction with the flat interest of a man three hours from the end of a shift, bagged it all without comment. "You can object as much as you'd like. The objection has no mechanism."

"You're a student who tests out of courses to save on tuition."

"I am, yes." She took the bag off the counter with both hands and looked up at him — five foot one to his six foot four, a difference that required her to tip her whole head back — and gave him the smallest shrug she owned, one shoulder, learned from him ten minutes ago and returned. "You held a fan cage steady for two hours with an arm that had already been cut open once. Come outside, there are chairs."

She turned and walked out under the chime without waiting to see whether he followed, which was the single most reckless thing she had done in some years, and she felt his shadow come up over her in the doorway before she was three steps across the lot.

---

The seating area was two plastic tables and four stacking chairs beside the ice cabinet, under the hard edge of the canopy light where it gave out into the dark. A standing ashtray held four hours of somebody's shift. The asphalt was wet in a long stripe where the day's pump drips had run downhill and never dried.

Lucy was lying against the base of the ice cabinet with her chin on her paws, and she came up off the ground when the door chimed and crossed to them at a businesslike trot with her whole back end going, and shoved her broad gold skull straight into Ami's hip hard enough to move her half a step.

"*Oh,*" said Ami, delighted, and went down onto her heels in her sleep shorts on wet asphalt and put both hands into the ruff behind the dog's ears without any further thought about the state of her legs or the state of anything.

Byl set the bag on the table and sat down. The plastic chair made a noise of genuine complaint.

"So there's a girl," he said.

Ami kept her eyes on the dog. "Mm."

"Model. Or — she is a model, that's her actual job." He got the coffee open and turned the can around in his hands without drinking any of it. "Met her in an alley about two weeks back, five men jumped me and she waded in without being asked and took two of them apart, which is its own thing I haven't sorted out. She's been texting since. Night before last she told me to come up on a roof in Yoyogi at eleven at night and be interesting at her for an hour."

"Did you?"

"I came off a job with a bucket in my hands covered in somebody's ceiling and went and sat on a parapet with her until two in the morning." He said it slowly, like a man reading his own police report. "And it was — it was good. I don't get that. Sitting on a roof with somebody talking about nothing. That's not a thing that happens in my week. It's a change of pace. It's nice." He turned the can another quarter. "Same as this."

Ami's hands kept moving in the dog's fur. Her heart did something arrhythmic and undignified and she permitted it, out here, in the dark, at the edge of the light.

"Same as this," she repeated.

"Yeah. Talking. Sitting. Nobody wants anything off me." He drank some of the coffee finally and made a face at it. "Anyway. The roof was flirting. It was flirting for about an hour and a half and I knew it and she knew it and I let it happen, and somewhere in there she told me she's got a boyfriend. Five months. Good one, apparently — I mean actually good, she listed his virtues, it was a whole inventory. And she's got — " He stopped and restarted. "She's got a lot of things she's working through. Her own stuff. Big stuff. She said so."

"So she has a boyfriend and unresolved matters," said Ami.

"That's the shape of it."

"I don't."

It was out of her before the sentence had been through any of the usual committees.

Byl looked over at her, crouched on the wet asphalt in her sleep shorts with her arms buried to the wrists in his dog, and Ami looked back with her chin up and her ears going hot and her whole considerable intellect standing to one side with its hands behind its back.

He smiled. It was a good smile. It reached his eyes and everything.

"Okay," he said.

And he had another drink of the coffee and looked out at the road.

Ami waited three full seconds.

*Ah,* she thought.

She revised. She was extremely good at revising; it was arguably the only skill she had that mattered. *Hypothesis one: he is being gracious, and declining to press an advantage. Rejected — he's not looking at me, he's looking at the road, and a man declining an advantage watches for the effect of the decline.*

*Hypothesis two: he is dense. A genuinely dense specimen. There exists, apparently, a species of male in which the signal arrives fully formed, in plain Japanese, from ten inches away, and simply does not decode.*

*Hypothesis three.* She thought about the laugh at the counter — the easy unbothered laugh of a man who had heard a small joke — and about *none of them lasted long*, and about five and a half years, and about the way he had said *nobody wants anything off me* as though it were a description of the weather.

*Hypothesis three: he decoded it perfectly and could not make it mean what it means, because the model he is running does not contain a term for a woman wanting him. So the data got thrown out as noise. Again.*

Three was correct. She knew it was correct the way she knew a proof was finished, all at once and without triumph.

It was going to require a great deal of work. It was going to require repetition, and unambiguity, and possibly a degree of directness that would humiliate her in ways she could not yet fully picture, and she was going to have to do all of it standing in a line she was now fairly sure had at least two other women in it already and one of them famous.

Ami Mizuno considered the size of the problem.

Then she scratched under Lucy's jaw with both hands and found the exact place that made the golden's back leg start going, and she began, methodically, to lay out a schedule.

"Tell me about the shrine," she said. "All eighteen items. Start at the top."

"You don't want to hear about a shrine roof at half twelve at night."

"I took a rooftop unit apart in thirty-four-degree heat for the entertainment value." She got up off her heels, dusted her palms on her thighs, and sat down in the plastic chair across from him, close enough that their knees were a hand's width apart under the table, and pushed the egg sandwich over to his side of it because he had eaten both pieces of chicken in the time it took her to run three hypotheses. "Start at the top. What's rotten underneath."

Byl started at the top.

Lucy shoved in between the two chairs, turned around twice, and lay down across both their feet with her tail going slow and steady against the wet asphalt — thump, thump, thump — in the last of the canopy light, and it went on like that for an hour and eleven minutes, and Ami did not once look at the time.
